{
 "thread": "QFyvk8NTlKY",
 "subject": "Towards Partial Initialization",
 "messages_listed": 4,
 "last_activity": "2015-02-17T00:13:05Z",
 "messages": [
  {
   "id": "i3A0TVwK8I8J",
   "from": "Michael Betancourt",
   "author": "a5e83218c834e013",
   "date": "2015-02-15T14:10:16Z",
   "subject": "Towards Partial Initialization",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "I’m trying to finalize a pattern/refactorization of the code that will admit\r<br>partial inits and a few other features we’ll need going forward.\r<br>\r<br>The first issue is maps to and from the unconstrained space.  Right\r<br>now they’re all exposed via stan::io::writer (maps constrained to\r<br>unconstrained) and stan::io::reader (maps unconstrained to\r<br>constrained).  These are classes with an internal value\r<br>accumulator — we’ll need to break this up somehow so that\r<br>we can do the maps one parameter (or set of parameters) at\r<br>a time.  I’m thinking either individual functions or functors,\r<br>either taking in vectors and returning vectors.  Thoughts?\r<br>\r<br>For partial inits we have the problem that the model and the\r<br>var_context are in some sense too abstracted and there’s no\r<br>easy way for the var_context to access the constrained-to-\r<br>unconstrained maps.  In his pull requrest, Jiqiang got around\r<br>this by having an external object that stored all of the model’s\r<br>parameters and their sizes but that adds a bunch of indirection.\r<br>I have two proposals.\r<br>\r<br>Firstly, let’s review how initialization currently works.  The\r<br>pattern is vaguely\r<br>\r<br>model::initialize(var_context) {\r<br>  for parameter in parameters {\r<br>    var_context::does_parameter_exist(parameter);\r<br>    values = var_context::get_values(parameter):\r<br>    var_context::verify_dimensions(parameter, dims);\r<br>    internal_parameters.append(map_to_unconstrained(values));\r<br>  }\r<br>}\r<br>\r<br>verify_dimensions will throw an exception if it fails, but the model\r<br>does not explicitly catch this.  An immediate simplification is to\r<br>combine the parameter check, dimension verification, and get\r<br>all into one function that throws an informative exception.\r<br>\r<br>Now there are two ways of doing random inits without needed\r<br>to figure out the global state of the model.  One option is to\r<br>encode random inits directly into the model,\r<br>\r<br>model::initialize(var_context) {\r<br>  for parameter in parameters {\r<br>    try {\r<br>      values = var_context::get_values(parameter, constrained_dim);  // combined check, verification, and get\r<br>      internal_parameters.append(map_to_unconstrained(values));\r<br>    } catch(var_context_exception) {\r<br>      internal_parameters.append(var_context.random_array(unconstrained_dim));\r<br>    }\r<br>  }\r<br>}\r<br>\r<br>The other option is to have the model pass transformation functors\r<br>to the var_context,\r<br>\r<br>model::initialize(var_context) {\r<br>  for parameter in parameters {\r<br>    internal_parameters.append(var_context.get_unconstrained&lt;map&gt;(parameter, constrained_dim));\r<br>  }\r<br>}\r<br>\r<br>where \r<br>\r<br>template &lt;Map&gt;\r<br>vector&lt;double&gt; var_context::get_unconstrained(parameter, constrained_dim) {\r<br>  if(does_parameter_exist(parameter) &amp;&amp; verify_dimensions(parameter, constrained_dim))\r<br>    return Map::unconstrain(get(parameter));\r<br>  else\r<br>    return random_array(Map::unconstrained_dim());\r<br>}\r<br>\r<br>and a Map functor looks like\r<br>\r<br>class map {\r<br>  static vector&lt;double&gt; constrain(vector&lt;double&gt;&amp; input);\r<br>  static vector&lt;double&gt; unconstrained(vector&lt;double&gt;&amp; input);\r<br>  static int constrained_dim();\r<br>  static int unconstrained_dim(); \r<br>}\r<br>\r<br>Thoughts?"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "W60tl3UdWq8J",
   "from": "Bob Carpenter",
   "author": "8aa9b8cc06fdc99c",
   "date": "2015-02-15T14:57:54Z",
   "subject": "Re: [stan-dev] Towards Partial Initialization",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "\r<br>&gt; On Feb 16, 2015, at 1:10 AM, Michael Betancourt &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">betan...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; I’m trying to finalize a pattern/refactorization of the code that will admit\r<br>&gt; partial inits and a few other features we’ll need going forward.\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; The first issue is maps to and from the unconstrained space.  Right\r<br>&gt; now they’re all exposed via stan::io::writer (maps constrained to\r<br>&gt; unconstrained) and stan::io::reader (maps unconstrained to\r<br>&gt; constrained).  These are classes with an internal value\r<br>&gt; accumulator — we’ll need to break this up somehow so that\r<br>&gt; we can do the maps one parameter (or set of parameters) at\r<br>&gt; a time.  I’m thinking either individual functions or functors,\r<br>&gt; either taking in vectors and returning vectors.  Thoughts?\r<br>\r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "The io::reader and io::writer classes don&#39;t know anything about\r<br>any specific Stan program, right?  When given data, they provide\r<br>pull-like methods to get variables.  The internal pointers they\r<br>maintain say where the next data to grab is (at least for the reader).\r<br>\r<br>Are you thinking of modifying the code generated for a model\r<br>class to provide a maping from variable names to dimensions and \r<br>constraint type?  That seems generally useful to me.\r<br>\r<br>Then we could map the constraint type to the functors to do\r<br>the transforms and inverse transforms (with and without the\r<br>Jacobians in the case of the inverse transforms).\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "\r<br>\r<br>&gt; For partial inits we have the problem that the model and the\r<br>&gt; var_context are in some sense too abstracted and there’s no\r<br>&gt; easy way for the var_context to access the constrained-to-\r<br>&gt; unconstrained maps.  In his pull requrest, Jiqiang got around\r<br>&gt; this by having an external object that stored all of the model’s\r<br>&gt; parameters and their sizes but that adds a bunch of indirection.\r<br>&gt; I have two proposals.\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Firstly, let’s review how initialization currently works.  The\r<br>&gt; pattern is vaguely\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; model::initialize(var_context) {\r<br>&gt;  for parameter in parameters {\r<br>&gt;    var_context::does_parameter_exist(parameter);\r<br>&gt;    values = var_context::get_values(parameter):\r<br>&gt;    var_context::verify_dimensions(parameter, dims);\r<br>&gt;    internal_parameters.append(map_to_unconstrained(values));\r<br>&gt;  }\r<br>&gt; }\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; verify_dimensions will throw an exception if it fails, but the model\r<br>&gt; does not explicitly catch this.  An immediate simplification is to\r<br>&gt; combine the parameter check, dimension verification, and get\r<br>&gt; all into one function that throws an informative exception.\r<br>\r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "That seems like a very good way to go.  It encapsulates what needs\r<br>to be encapsulated and takes some burden off the code generator.\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "\r<br>\r<br>&gt; Now there are two ways of doing random inits without needed\r<br>&gt; to figure out the global state of the model.  One option is to\r<br>&gt; encode random inits directly into the model,\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; model::initialize(var_context) {\r<br>&gt;  for parameter in parameters {\r<br>&gt;    try {\r<br>&gt;      values = var_context::get_values(parameter, constrained_dim);  // combined check, verification, and get\r<br>&gt;      internal_parameters.append(map_to_unconstrained(values));\r<br>&gt;    } catch(var_context_exception) {\r<br>&gt;      internal_parameters.append(var_context.random_array(unconstrained_dim));\r<br>&gt;    }\r<br>&gt;  }\r<br>&gt; }\r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "It&#39;s bad news to use try/catch for normal courses\r<br>of execution.  It makes the code both inefficient (exception\r<br>dispatching and catching is expensive because it needs to be\r<br>general enough to blow through arbitrary functions) and\r<br>inscrutable (exceptions aren&#39;t really exceptions).\r<br>\r<br>I like the Javascript (or Java properties) way of specifying\r<br>this kind of thing that lets you query whether something is available \r<br>from the primary resource (specified inits), and if not, get it\r<br>from a secondary resource (random inits).\r<br>\r<br>I don&#39;t see why this would be encoded in the model --- which\r<br>bits are specific to the model?\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "\r<br>&gt; The other option is to have the model pass transformation functors\r<br>&gt; to the var_context,\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; model::initialize(var_context) {\r<br>&gt;  for parameter in parameters {\r<br>&gt;    internal_parameters.append(var_context.get_unconstrained&lt;map&gt;(parameter, constrained_dim));\r<br>&gt;  }\r<br>&gt; }\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; where \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; template &lt;Map&gt;\r<br>&gt; vector&lt;double&gt; var_context::get_unconstrained(parameter, constrained_dim) {\r<br>&gt;  if(does_parameter_exist(parameter) &amp;&amp; verify_dimensions(parameter, constrained_dim))\r<br>&gt;    return Map::unconstrain(get(parameter));\r<br>&gt;  else\r<br>&gt;    return random_array(Map::unconstrained_dim());\r<br>&gt; }\r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "I think I like this second approach better.  Basically, the\r<br>compiled program class makes a method available to map variable\r<br>names to types (including constraints).  Then the data determines\r<br>the sizes.  Then when we init, we use those two things.\r<br>\r<br>Also, I think we&#39;ll need to pass in bounds for the random_array if\r<br>we want to do anything other than uniform on (-2,2).  \r<br>\r<br>Marcus had suggested generalizing beyond uniform at one point,\r<br>but it&#39;s never seemed useful to me.   But maybe I&#39;m forgetting\r<br>some motivation.\r<br>\r<br>By the way, another alternative, also suggested by Marcus,\r<br>would be to have a block in the Stan program that specifies\r<br>inits.  It&#39;d be nice to have the design be flexible enough that\r<br>we can do that at some point if we want.  It&#39;d be nice to keep\r<br>the random init code and the model together.  Or maybe not?\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "\r<br>&gt; and a Map functor looks like\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; class map {\r<br>&gt;  static vector&lt;double&gt; constrain(vector&lt;double&gt;&amp; input);\r<br>&gt;  static vector&lt;double&gt; unconstrained(vector&lt;double&gt;&amp; input);\r<br>&gt;  static int constrained_dim();\r<br>&gt;  static int unconstrained_dim(); \r<br>&gt; }\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Thoughts?\r<br>\r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "The issue in my mind is whether we do this all with vectors\r<br>or whetner we add more structure.  I don&#39;t know which way will play out\r<br>to be easier.\r<br>\r<br>If we have some JSON file (or R dump file), then it&#39;s going to\r<br>define some variables for initialization on the constrained scale.\r<br>\r<br>The sampler is going to need to pull out values for all variables\r<br>during initialization.  It makes sense to me to have it first\r<br>look in whatever wraps the input file, then if it doesn&#39;t find it,\r<br>do what you suggest above.\r<br>\r<br>Sorry if this isn&#39;t all coherent.  I&#39;m trying to respond so you&#39;re\r<br>not blocked waiting for comments, but it&#39;s late here (I&#39;m not back\r<br>to my regular schedule yet!).\r<br>\r<br>- Bob\r<br>\r<br>\r<br>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
